page("two-zone-fire-on-a-small-grill","How to Build a Two-Zone Fire on a Small Charcoal Grill","The one technique that makes small grills easy: hot side, cool side, and how to use them for steaks, chicken, burgers and griddle cooks on the Slot Grill Compact.",IMG["c08"],f"""
<p>On a big cooker you can get away with an even bed of coals and a lid. On a 16.25" × 9" open charcoal grill like the Slot Grill Compact, an even, wall-to-wall bed of coals means everything on the grate cooks at one speed, and that speed is usually "too fast." Thin food is fine. Anything thick, fatty or bone-in chars outside before the middle is done. The fix is the most useful habit in small-grill cooking: the <b>two-zone fire</b>.</p>
<p>A two-zone fire is simply a hot side and a cool side in the same firebox. You sear where the coals are deep, then move food to where they are thin or absent to finish gently, hold, or escape a flare-up. It costs nothing, needs no extra gear, and it turns a compact grill from a burger-only machine into something that handles ribeyes, chicken thighs, sausages and a griddle breakfast at the same time.</p>
<h2>Why small grills need it more than big ones</h2><ul><li><b>The food sits close to the fire.</b> The Compact is 10.5" tall overall, so the grate is close to the coals. That's great for searing, and it's why heat control comes from <i>where</i> the coals are rather than how high the food sits.</li><li><b>There's no lid to roast with.</b> On an open grill, the cool side is your "oven": food there cooks mostly from gentle radiant heat and the warm steel under it.</li><li><b>Fat drips straight onto coals.</b> Chicken skin, burgers and sausages will flare. A place to move them is better than a squirt bottle, which kicks up ash onto your food.</li><li><b>Space is precious.</b> A cool zone doubles as a holding shelf, so finished food stays warm while the next batch cooks.</li></ul>
<h2>Step by step: building the two zones</h2><ol><li><b>Light the coals</b> in a chimney starter (see <a href="/how-to-light-charcoal-without-lighter-fluid.html">lighting without lighter fluid</a>). For a weeknight cook for two, a half chimney is usually enough on this size grill. For a long cook or a crowd, fill it.</li><li><b>Wait until the top coals are lightly ashed over.</b> Pouring coals that are still black on top gives you a weak, smoky start.</li><li><b>Pour toward one end.</b> Bank the lit coals across roughly two-thirds of the firebox length. Use tongs to push them into an even, slightly deeper pile at the far end.</li><li><b>Leave the last third thin or empty.</b> A few scattered coals there give a "medium" zone; none gives a true cool zone.</li><li><b>Set the grates</b> and let them heat for a few minutes. Hot steel releases food better and gives cleaner grill marks.</li><li><b>Clean and oil the grates</b> with a folded paper towel dipped in high-smoke-point oil, held in tongs.</li><li><b>Test the zones with your hand.</b> Hold your palm about where the food will sit. If you have to pull away in about 2 seconds, that's high heat; 4–5 seconds is medium; 7 or more is low. It's a rough guide, but it's quick and it works anywhere.</li></ol>
<h2>Three layouts worth knowing</h2><div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Layout</th><th>How</th><th>Best for</th></tr><tr><td>Classic two-zone</td><td>Coals across two-thirds, cool third empty</td><td>Steaks, chops, chicken pieces, burgers, sausages</td></tr><tr><td>Three-zone (hot / medium / off)</td><td>Deep pile at one end, single layer in the middle, empty at the other end</td><td>Mixed cooks: sear, finish and hold at the same time</td></tr><tr><td>Even bed</td><td>Coals spread wall to wall in one layer</td><td>Thin, quick foods and griddle work: smash burgers, eggs, shrimp, skewers</td></tr></table></div>
<h2>How to use each zone</h2><ul><li><b>Sear</b> over the deep coals, then slide thicker cuts to the cool end to finish. This is the backbone of the <a href="/reverse-sear-steak-small-grill.html">ribeye</a> and <a href="/pork-chops-small-charcoal-grill.html">pork chop</a> recipes.</li><li><b>Start fatty food on the cool side.</b> Bone-in, skin-on <a href="/chicken-thighs-small-charcoal-grill.html">chicken thighs</a> render more gently there, then crisp over the coals at the end.</li><li><b>Rescue flare-ups</b> by moving food to the cool end until the flames die down.</li><li><b>Hold</b> finished burgers, sausages or <a href="/chicken-wings-small-charcoal-grill.html">wings</a> at the cool end while the next round cooks.</li><li><b>Warm</b> tortillas, buns and bread at the cool end for the last minute.</li></ul>
<h2>Two zones with a griddle plate</h2><p>The Compact's top is two pieces, so you can run a grate over the hot side and a <a href="{GRID}">Slot Grill griddle (plancha)</a> over the other, or a full griddle setup. The griddle is made from the same thick carbon steel as the grill and is designed to flat-stack with it. Steaks on the grate, onions, peppers or eggs on the flat top, one fire. Put the griddle over the side with more coals if you want it hot for searing smash burgers, or over the thinner side for eggs and pancakes.</p>
<h2>Keeping the zones going</h2><ul><li>Add a few fresh coals to the hot side every 20–30 minutes on a long cook. Lump catches faster; briquettes add steadier heat once lit.</li><li>For waves of food, keep a second chimney lighting on a safe surface so you can top up with lit coals instead of cold ones.</li><li>Push the coals together with tongs as they burn down. Spread-out coals lose heat quickly.</li><li>Wind pulls heat away from the downwind side. Point the hot end into the wind or set up behind a vehicle or wall (with safe clearance).</li></ul>
<h2>Troubleshooting</h2><div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Likely cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr><tr><td>Outside burnt, inside raw</td><td>Too much time over high heat</td><td>Sear, then finish on the cool side; use a thermometer</td></tr><tr><td>Constant flare-ups</td><td>Fat dripping onto the hot zone</td><td>Start fatty food on the cool side; trim excess fat; move food, don't spray water</td></tr><tr><td>No sear, food sticks</td><td>Coals not ready, grates not hot, or food wet</td><td>Wait for ashed-over coals, preheat grates, pat food dry</td></tr><tr><td>Fire dies mid-cook</td><td>Coals spread too thin, damp charcoal, or ash buildup</td><td>Consolidate coals, top up with lit coals, keep charcoal dry, empty ash between cooks</td></tr><tr><td>Cool side is still too hot</td><td>Coals crept across the firebox</td><td>Push them back with tongs; leave a wider empty strip</td></tr></table></div>
<h2>Doneness and food safety</h2><p>A two-zone fire makes it easy to hit your target temperature, but you still need to check it.</p>
{TEMPS}
{QUICK}
{MISTAKES}
""","fire",[("What is a two-zone fire?","A charcoal fire with a hot side (deep coals) and a cool side (thin or no coals) in the same grill, so you can sear over direct heat and finish or hold food over gentler heat."),("How much charcoal do I need for a two-zone fire on the Slot Grill Compact?","For a quick cook for one or two, a half chimney of briquettes is usually plenty. For longer cooks or groups, start with a full chimney and add a few coals to the hot side as you go."),("Can I use a griddle with a two-zone fire?","Yes. The Compact's top is two pieces, so you can run a grate on one side and a Slot Grill griddle (plancha) on the other, placing the griddle over more or fewer coals depending on how hot you want it."),("How do I stop flare-ups without water?","Move the food to the cool side until the flames die down. Spraying water kicks ash onto the food and cools the fire unevenly.")])

page("best-charcoal-for-small-grills","Charcoal vs. Wood on a Small Grill: What to Burn","Briquettes, lump charcoal or cooking wood in the Slot Grill Compact: how each one lights, burns and tastes, how much you need, and how to store fuel.",IMG["c28"],f"""
<p>The Slot Grill Compact burns charcoal or cooking wood. Both work, but they behave differently in a small open firebox, and picking the right one for the cook makes a bigger difference on a 146 sq in grill than on a big one. Here's what to burn, how much, and how to handle it.</p>
<h2>The three fuels at a glance</h2><div class="tbl"><table><tr><th></th><th>Briquettes</th><th>Lump charcoal</th><th>Cooking wood</th></tr><tr><td>Lighting</td><td>Slower; needs time to ash over</td><td>Faster</td><td>Needs kindling; burns down to coals</td></tr><tr><td>Heat</td><td>Steady, even</td><td>Hotter, more variable</td><td>Varies with species and dryness</td></tr><tr><td>Burn time</td><td>Longer</td><td>Shorter</td><td>Shortest; feed as you go</td></tr><tr><td>Ash</td><td>More</td><td>Less</td><td>Some</td></tr><tr><td>Piece size</td><td>Uniform</td><td>Mixed, from dust to big chunks</td><td>Whatever you split</td></tr><tr><td>Best for</td><td>Chicken, sausages, longer and group cooks</td><td>Hard steak sears, quick cooks</td><td>Flavor, campfire-style cooking, after-dinner fire</td></tr></table></div>
<h2>Briquettes</h2><p>Briquettes are compressed charcoal with binders, shaped for uniform size. In a small firebox that uniformity is a real advantage: they stack evenly, burn at a predictable rate, and make <a href="/charcoal-temperature-control-small-grill.html">temperature control</a> easier. They do make more ash, which matters on a compact grill because ash sitting under the coals chokes airflow. Empty it between cooks.</p><p>Let briquettes fully ash over in the chimney before pouring. Half-lit briquettes smell and taste off and give weak heat.</p>
<h2>Lump charcoal</h2><p>Lump is wood that's been charred into charcoal without binders. It lights quickly, burns hotter, and leaves less ash, which is why many people like it for steak. The tradeoffs: it burns faster, pieces are uneven (break big chunks so they sit flat), and the bag usually has dust at the bottom. Leave the dust in the bag; it smothers airflow.</p>
<h2>Cooking wood</h2><p>Untreated, seasoned hardwood splits work in the Compact too. Build a small fire, let it burn down to glowing coals, then cook over the coals rather than the flames. Wood gives the most flavor and the most campfire feel, but it takes longer to be ready and needs feeding. Keep splits small enough to fit inside the firebox without hanging over the sides.</p><p>Many cooks mix fuels: a bed of charcoal for steady heat with a small split or a few wood chunks added for flavor.</p>
<h2>How much fuel?</h2><div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Cook</th><th>Starting fuel (guide)</th></tr><tr><td>Quick weeknight for 1–2 (burgers, a steak, sausages)</td><td>About a half chimney</td></tr><tr><td>Bone-in chicken, chops, wings, or two waves of food</td><td>A fuller chimney, plus a few coals added to the hot side later</td></tr><tr><td><a href="/cooking-for-a-crowd-small-grill.html">Cooking for a crowd</a></td><td>A full chimney to start, with a second chimney lighting for top-ups</td></tr><tr><td>Griddle-only breakfast</td><td>A modest, even bed under the griddle</td></tr></table></div><p>These are starting points. Wind, cold weather and damp fuel all mean you need more. It's always easier to add lit coals than to take heat away on a small grill.</p>
<h2>Storing fuel</h2><ul><li>Keep charcoal dry. Damp charcoal is miserable to light and burns weakly. A lidded bin or a dry bag works for trips.</li><li>Roll the bag closed after use.</li><li>Store away from the grill area and any heat source.</li></ul>
<h2>Never burn</h2><p>Treated, painted or stained lumber, pallets of unknown origin, plywood, trash, plastic, or anything you can't identify. They can release toxic fumes onto your food. Use only real charcoal or untreated cooking wood, and skip lighter fluid (it flares in wind and can taint food). See <a href="/how-to-light-charcoal-without-lighter-fluid.html">how to light without it</a>.</p>
{QUICK}
<h2>Fuel for specific cooks</h2><div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Cook</th><th>Suggested fuel</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td><a href="/reverse-sear-steak-small-grill.html">Ribeye</a>, <a href="/steak-fajitas-compact-grill.html">skirt steak</a></td><td>Lump, or a full chimney of briquettes</td><td>Maximum sear heat</td></tr><tr><td><a href="/chicken-thighs-small-charcoal-grill.html">Chicken thighs</a>, <a href="/chicken-wings-small-charcoal-grill.html">wings</a></td><td>Briquettes</td><td>Steady heat for 40+ minutes</td></tr><tr><td><a href="/smash-burgers-on-a-griddle.html">Smash burgers</a></td><td>Either, spread evenly under the griddle</td><td>Even, hot flat-top</td></tr><tr><td><a href="/grilled-fish-compact-grill.html">Fish</a>, <a href="/grilled-shrimp-compact-grill.html">shrimp</a></td><td>Half chimney of either</td><td>Short, hot cook</td></tr><tr><td><a href="/campfire-breakfast-griddle.html">Breakfast</a></td><td>Modest bed of briquettes</td><td>Gentle, even griddle heat</td></tr><tr><td><a href="/smores-and-grilled-desserts-compact-grill.html">S'mores</a>, after-dinner fire</td><td>Cooking wood on the last coals</td><td>Flames and atmosphere where allowed</td></tr></table></div>
<h2>Reading your coals</h2><ul><li><b>Black with flames:</b> still lighting. Wait.</li><li><b>Red glow, light gray ash:</b> peak heat. Sear now.</li><li><b>Thick white ash, dim glow:</b> fading. Good for gentle cooking, desserts, or time to add fuel.</li><li><b>Small and crumbling:</b> nearly spent. Consolidate or let the fire burn out.</li></ul>
<h2>Smoke and flavor</h2><p>Clean-burning, fully lit charcoal gives a mild, pleasant smoke. Thick white or acrid smoke usually means unlit fuel, fat dripping on coals, or damp wood. A small chunk of untreated hardwood on hot coals adds aroma for short cooks.</p>{SAFETY}
""","fire",[("Can the Slot Grill Compact burn wood?","Yes. Slot Grill lists charcoal or cooking wood as fuel for the Compact. Use untreated, seasoned hardwood and cook over the coals once the flames die down."),("Is lump charcoal or briquettes better for a small grill?","Briquettes give steadier, longer, more predictable heat; lump lights faster, burns hotter and leaves less ash. Many cooks use briquettes for chicken and long cooks, and lump for steak sears."),("How much charcoal do I need?","About a half chimney is a good start for a quick cook for one or two on the Compact. Use more for bone-in meats, groups, wind or cold weather."),("Can I mix wood and charcoal?","Yes. A charcoal bed with a few hardwood chunks or a small split gives steady heat plus wood flavor.")])
